{
  "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
  "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0000-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:06 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Eric J. Feigin",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FEIGIN: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: There's no dispute that the district judge's finding by a preponderance of the evidence that Respondent possessed child pornography was constitutionally sufficient to revoke his supervised release and reimprison him under Section 3583(e)(3)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Is there any other area of the law in which we permit imprisonment by a preponderance of the evidence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Feigin",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The first thing I would say is, again, because this is a context in which the Sixth Amendment doesn't apply, I don't think Apprendi would by its own force apply."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Feigin",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The second thing is that the jury's verdict authorizes reimprisonment under (k) just the same as the conceded authorization of reimprisonment under (e)(3)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Feigin",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0001-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the fourth thing I would say, just very quickly, Your Honor, is there is no additional fact finding that is required under (k). The exact same finding of fact that he possessed child pornography by a preponderance of the evidence is the same finding of fact that would lead to revocation under (e)(3) as to revocation under (k)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lunn",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What 3583(k) does is that it stretches what the original conviction -- the -- the amount of sentence that was authorized by the jury's verdict in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, can I ask you about Alleyne? What Alleyne held is that the touchstone for determining whether a fact must be found by a jury beyond a reasonable doubt is whether the fact constitutes an element of the charged offense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lunn",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0001-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They -- well, the conditions that are imposed are not unlike a contempt situation. You're told to do certain things, and if you don't do those certain things in a court order, then -- then you may be found in contempt or, in this instance, you -- you may even be sentenced to prison, if necessary, for a very limited period of time, so long as it still is designed to reintegrate the former prisoner back into the community and is designed for rehabilitation purposes."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So how does that get turned into a regime that protects the prerogatives of a judge to decide what the term of imprisonment should be?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lunn",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "fa2afda9ecdd54f12884b7a045852688847a0464138a95fcfd2f3945949e18c4",
      "evidence_id": "ev-fa2afda9ecdd-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that there's a problem with the mandatory five, because it ought to be up to the discretion of the district judge."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2018-17-1672",
      "confidence": 0.0,
